      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) and identify its repeating decimal pattern. Once we have the repeating block, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit in the repeating sequence.\n\nFirst, let's perform the long division of 1 by 7.\n\n1. 1 ÷ 7 = 0 with a remainder of 1.\n2. Bring down the next 0, so we have 10.\n3. 10 ÷ 7 = 1 with a remainder of 3.\n4. Bring down the next 0, so we have 30.\n5. 30 ÷ 7 = 4 with a remainder of 2.\n6. Bring down the next 0, so we have 20.\n7. 20 ÷ 7 = 2 with a remainder of 6.\n8. Bring down the next 0, so we have 60.\n9. 60 ÷ 7 = 8 with a remainder of 4.\n10. Bring down the next 0, so we have 40.\n11. 40 ÷ 7 = 5 with a remainder of 5.\n12. Bring down the next 0, so we have 50.\n13. 50 ÷ 7 = 7 with a remainder of 1, and the process repeats from step 1.\n\nSo, the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\( 0.\\overline{142857} \\). This means the digits 142857 repeat indefinitely.\n\nTo find the 100th digit, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating block. Since the block \"142857\" has a length of 6, we can find the position by calculating the remainder when 100 is divided by 6.\n\n\\[ 100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\n\nThe quotient is 16, which indicates that 16 full cycles of the block have been completed, and we are 4 digits into the next cycle. The 4th digit of the repeating block \"142857\" is 8.\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
